  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nThe mid/upper ridge continues to influence the weather across \neastern Kansas while a sfc trough is bisecting central Kansas. A \nwarm front extended ENE from the sfc trough across northeast KS/SE \nNE. South of this front, hot conditions within a deeply mixed pbl \nare progged to persist into Fri-Sat although the front may settle a \nbit further south leading to low confidence in the temperature \nforecast as we move into Friday. Subtle convergence within the \nsfc trough axis could allow for or isolated or widely scattered\nthunderstorms this afternoon and evening with the highest \nprobabilities across our central KS counties. Any storm that \ndevelops may be capable of producing strong/damaging winds given\nthe deeply mixed pbl/inverted-v profiles. Some storm activity \ncould fester overnight along the I-70 corridor in central KS as \nthe LLJ develops tonight but most locations across southern and \nsoutheast KS are expected to remain dry.\n\nAnother round of storms is expected on Friday evening and Friday \nnight along and north of the effective front/trough axis which may \nbe forced south of the anticipated location in central KS. Areas \nsouth of the effective front will see highs in the 100-105 degree \nrange with more seasonable temperatures in it's wake. This frontal \nzone may shift northward on Saturday with another hot day \nanticipated for much of the area. Showers and thunderstorms will \nremain possible, especially during the evening and overnight \nhours along and north of this boundary.\n\nBy late Saturday night into Sunday, the frontal zone is expected \nshift southward once again ushering in more seasonable air for \nSunday-Monday with continued chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThe mid/upper ridge is progged to amplify over the Southern Rockies \nearly in the week before translating eastward into the \nSouthern/Central Plains by midweek. Hot and dry weather may return \nalthough some low pops were maintained through the period as we see \na couple of decent shortwave troughs racing eastward across the \nNorthern Plains which may shift the frontal zone further south than \nprogged.",
